From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Carl Su <xuyusu@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Mon, 25 May 2026 16:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] json_tokener: disable uselocale path on uClibc

json-c 0.18 uses duplocale(uselocale(NULL)) before parsing JSON when
HAVE_USELOCALE is detected.  With this uClibc toolchain, uselocale(NULL)
returns LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE ((locale_t)-1), and duplocale() dereferences that
sentinel and segfaults under qemu-arm.

uClibc also provides setlocale(), so force json_tokener.c to use the existing
setlocale fallback path on uClibc targets.  This keeps JSON parsing in the C
numeric locale without calling the broken duplocale() implementation, and
fixes all target applications linked against libjson-c.

Signed-off-by: Carl Su <xuyusu@qti.qualcomm.com>
---
 json_tokener.c | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/json_tokener.c b/json_tokener.c
index 2d0d5d4..8d9b10d 100644
--- a/json_tokener.c
+++ b/json_tokener.c
@@ -41,6 +41,20 @@
 #include <strings.h>
 #endif /* HAVE_STRINGS_H */
 
+/*
+ * uClibc exposes uselocale()/duplocale(), so json-c's configure enables
+ * HAVE_USELOCALE.  However, this toolchain's uselocale(NULL) returns the
+ * LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE sentinel ((locale_t)-1), and duplocale() dereferences it,
+ * causing json_tokener_parse_ex() to segfault under qemu-arm.
+ *
+ * Use json-c's existing setlocale() fallback on uClibc instead.  That still
+ * forces LC_NUMERIC to "C" while parsing, but avoids the broken duplocale()
+ * path for every target application using libjson-c.
+ */
+#if defined(__UCLIBC__) && defined(HAVE_USELOCALE)
+#undef HAVE_USELOCALE
+#endif
+
 #define jt_hexdigit(x) (((x) <= '9') ? (x) - '0' : ((x)&7) + 9)
 
 #if !HAVE_STRNCASECMP && defined(_MSC_VER)
-- 
2.34.1
